<BaseLayout
  title="2027 401(k) Super Catch-Up for Ages 60–63 ($11,750 Limit)"
  description="How the SECURE 2.0 super catch-up works in 2027: $11,750 replaces the $8,500 catch-up for ages 60, 61, 62, and 63, bringing the total employee limit to $37,250."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>2027 401(k) Super Catch-Up for Ages 60–63</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Employees who attain age 60, 61, 62, or 63 by December 31, 2027 can contribute a <strong>$11,750</strong> super catch-up on top of the $25,500 base limit, for a total of <strong>$37,250</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-back">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/" style="text-decoration: none;">Calculate Your Ages 60–63 Paycheck →</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Replaces—Does Not Stack With—the $8,500 Catch-Up</h2>
    <p>
      Under Internal Revenue Code Section 414(v)(2)(B)(i), the $11,750 super catch-up <strong>replaces</strong> the standard $8,500 catch-up for ages 60–63. You cannot add $11,750 and $8,500 together.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Under 50:</strong> $25,500 total</li>
      <li><strong>Ages 50–59:</strong> $25,500 + $8,500 = $34,000 total</li>
      <li><strong>Ages 60–63:</strong> $25,500 + $11,750 = $37,250 total</li>
      <li><strong>Age 64+:</strong> $25,500 + $8,500 = $34,000 total (drops back to standard catch-up)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Per-Paycheck Max-Out Amounts for $37,250</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Biweekly (26 paychecks):</strong> $1,432.69 per paycheck</li>
      <li><strong>Biweekly (27 paychecks):</strong> $1,379.62 regular + $1,379.88 final</li>
      <li><strong>Weekly (52 paychecks):</strong> $716.34 regular + $716.66 final</li>
      <li><strong>Semi-monthly (24 paychecks):</strong> $1,552.08 per paycheck</li>
      <li><strong>Monthly (12 paychecks):</strong> $3,104.16 regular + $3,104.24 final</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

